findings.body:
```
try {
    await sendAddGuestsEmails(evt, guests);
} catch (err) {
    console.log("Error sending AddGuestsEmails");
}
```
The actual error (`err`) is discarded and only a generic string is logged via `console.log` rather than the project's structured logger. This makes it impossible to diagnose email delivery failures in production and is inconsistent with the rest of the codebase, which generally uses `@calcom/lib/logger`.

```
onError: (err) => {
      const message = `${err.data?.code}: ${t(err.message)}`;
      showToast(message || t("unable_to_add_guests"), "error");
},
```
`message` is built from a template string so it is always truthy, meaning the `t("unable_to_add_guests")` fallback can never actually be shown. Additionally, `t(err.message)` passes the raw TRPC error message (which may not be a translation key, e.g. generic validation errors) directly into the translation function, which can display an un-translated raw string to users.
